---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import { nav } from '../../data/site';

const parts = nav[0].items.slice(1);
---
<Page
  title="Product"
  description="How ArborSpace fits together: an ontology, rules, a review queue, deployed on your premises."
  eyebrow="Product"
  heading="Expert knowledge the system can apply and people can review."
  lead="ArborSpace is not a chatbot over your documents, nor a better search, nor a data platform for dashboards. It captures the essence of your business in an ontology."
>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What an ontology is</h2>
        <p>
          An ontology is an operational layer for the organisation. It sits on top of the data
          already integrated (datasets, virtual tables and models) and holds both the semantics,
          meaning the objects, their properties and the links between them, and the actions and
          logic that apply to them.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Specific to each business</h2>
        <p>
          An ontology is specific to a business. It is built from the firm’s own vocabulary: the
          objects its experts talk about, the way those objects are connected, and the checks they
          make on them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The building blocks</p>
        <h2>Four parts, one memory.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="cards">
        {parts.map((p) => (
          <a class="card" href={p.href}>
            <h3>{p.label}</h3>
            <p>{p.description}</p>
            <span class="more">Learn more →</span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
